<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip II of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1165">1165</a>–<a href="/years/1223">1223</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Philip Augustus · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">Capetian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, known as Philip Augustus, was king of France from 1180 to 1223. He conquered Normandy, Anjou, Maine and Poitou from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> from 1204 and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bouvines">Battle of Bouvines</a> in 1214, breaking the Angevin Empire. His reign greatly enlarged the royal domain and made the Capetian monarchy the strongest power in France.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france.jpg" alt="Philip II of France" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sceau_de_Philippe_Auguste._-_Archives_Nationales_-_SC-D157.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Crusade (1189–1192) revealed the limits of Ayyubid power against determined European opponents: Saladin lost Acre to the combined forces of Richard I of England and <b>Philip II of France</b> in 1191 and was defeated in the field at Arsuf, though he retained Jerusalem under the Treaty of Jaffa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1204 King <b>Philip II of France</b> conquered continental Normandy from King John of England, but the Channel Islands, which John’s forces held or soon recovered with the help of the English fleet, remained with the English crown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bailiwick-of-jersey">Bailiwick of Jersey · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Count Ferrand was captured at the Battle of Bouvines in 1214, fighting in the coalition of Otto IV and King John of England against <b>Philip II Augustus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Baldwin V (r. 1171–1195) fought his neighbours, among them Brabant and Limburg, to secure the inheritance of Namur, and backed his son-in-law <b>Philip II Augustus of France</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After Eleanor's death in 1204 King <b>Philip II Augustus</b> overran Normandy and Anjou, and the French crown took Poitou in stages, completing the conquest with the fall of La Rochelle in 1224.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Henry II’s son Geoffrey ruled through his marriage to the heiress Constance, and after their son Arthur of Brittany disappeared in King John’s custody around 1203, <b>Philip II of France</b> gave the duchy to Peter Mauclerc of the house of Dreux in 1213.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Mantes-la-Jolie, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1202" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Philip II of France"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1202">What was here in 1202? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
